        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Vedic civilisation</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>: first the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>, then from c. 1200–1000 BCE the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> Plain (the Kuru-Panchala region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Ganga-Yamuna Doab</a>), later Videha in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1500–500 BCE: Early Vedic c. 1500–1000 BCE (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> composed c. 1400–1000 BCE); Later Vedic c. 1000–600 BCE (the Kuru, Panchala and Videha kingdoms); the Sutra texts to c. 500 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>; fire sacrifice (yajna) and Śrauta ritual; the gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, Agni and Soma; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a>; the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>; the Ashvamedha horse sacrifice; the roots of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> and karma</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Vedic texts themselves, composed and orally transmitted with precision; the study of their language in five chronological layers; archaeological cultures linked with them (Ochre Coloured Pottery, Gandhara grave, Black and Red Ware, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware</a>); settlement surveys</span></div>
        </div>
