    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Vedic period takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, liturgical texts composed and passed on by word of mouth in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> between the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> cities and new cities of c. 600 BCE.</li><li>Early Vedic society was tribal and pastoral, centred in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>; later, with iron tools, it settled the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> Plain and formed kingdoms such as the Kuru.</li><li>The age produced fire sacrifice, the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, and its tradition became one of the main parts of the later “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> synthesis”.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Horses, needed for cavalry and for sacrifice, could not be bred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, so Vedic leaders kept up trade routes beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> to obtain them.</p></aside>
    </section>

