    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1900 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> collapses; in the mainstream view, Indo-Aryan groups then settle the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1400–1000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, the oldest layer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, is composed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early Vedic period</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatas">Bharatas</a> under their chief Sudas defeat a confederation of ten tribes in the Battle of the Ten Kings on the river Parushni (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a>)</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1200–1000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Vedic culture spreads east to the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> Plain, and iron tools come into use</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 12th–11th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a> forms; Vedic hymns are collected and the Śrauta rituals developed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 900–750 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After the Kuru decline, the political centre of Vedic culture moves to Panchala under King Keśin Dālbhya</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">8th or 7th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Videha rises under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janaka">Janaka</a>, patron of the sages <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a>, Uddalaka Aruni and Gargi Vachaknavi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 600–500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Mahajanapadas, the first true cities and the śramaṇa movements, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, mark the end of the period</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vedas.jpg" alt="Vedas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda,_manuscript_page_sample_i,_Mandala_1,_Hymn_1_(Sukta_1),_Adhyaya_1,_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ms Sarah Welch, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Vedic period, or Vedic age (c. 1500–c. 500 BCE), is the part of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> when the Vedic literature, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> (c. 1500–900 BCE), was composed. It falls between the end of the urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> and a second urbanisation that began in the central Indo-Gangetic Plain around 600 BCE. The Vedas are liturgical texts, composed and orally transmitted with precision by speakers of an Old Indo-Aryan language; together with the archaeological record they are the primary sources for the period.</p><p>In the account of the academic mainstream, groups of Indo-Aryan peoples migrated into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> Civilisation around 1900 BCE and settled the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> Valley. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-aryans">Indo-Aryans</a> are seen as a branch that diverged from other Indo-Iranian tribes at the Andronovo horizon, which arose from the Sintashta culture, and that moved through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> area of present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Their religion was closely related to the reconstructed Proto-Indo-European and Indo-Iranian religions; according to Anthony it also borrowed from the Bactria–Margiana culture, including the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and the ritual drink Soma. Some Indian writers and archaeologists reject a migration and argue for an indigenous origin of the Indo-Aryans, in an unbroken tradition reaching back to the Indus (Sindhu-Sarasvati) tradition of 7000 or 8000 BCE; this view is popular in India but outside the academic mainstream.</p><p>A much older chronology comes from post-Vedic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, in which the Vedas were received by the seven rishis thousands of years ago and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> War is dated to about 3100 BCE. Historians date the early Vedic age to the second half of the second millennium BCE, reconstructing its history from details within the texts, whose language falls into five chronological layers, correlated with archaeology.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cattle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cattle.jpg" alt="Cattle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bulgarian_gray_cattle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dr. Plamen Ivanov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Early Vedic people were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> society centred in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, organised into tribes rather than kingdoms and living mainly by herding. The economy combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> and agriculture: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> mentions levelling fields, processing seed and storing grain in large jars, and exchange ran through gifts to kings (bali) and priests (dana) and barter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> as a unit of value. Clans as a whole held rights over land and herds. After about 1000 BCE iron axes and ploughs made it possible to clear the forests of the Gangetic plains; Vedic settlements spread into the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Ganga-Yamuna Doab</a>, growing wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and barley, and new crafts arose, among them carpentry, tanning, pottery, jewellery, dyeing and winemaking.</p><p>Society was patriarchal and patrilineal. Early on the tribal chief (rajan) and nobility (rajanya) stood at the top, the common people (viś) in the middle and the dasa and dasyu at the bottom; the Rigveda does not yet link the words Brahmana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">Vaishya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a> are absent. In the later period, as Kulke and Rothermund describe it, four estates emerged: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests and warrior nobility on top, free peasants and traders third, and slaves, labourers and artisans, many from pre-Aryan groups, fourth. The later texts fixed roles and ritual purity for each, though they rank the top three inconsistently, and some hymns still suggest social mobility.</p><p>Women could choose their husbands and could remarry if their husbands died or disappeared, and women sages and goddesses were known, though a wife was subordinate to her husband; in the later period women were relegated to more subordinate roles, while the texts still name them as herders, weavers and dyers, the woman warrior Vishpala and two female philosophers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>. Tribal councils, the sabha and samiti, limited the early rajan; with the later kingdoms hereditary kingship emerged and the councils declined. Pastimes included music, dancing, drama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> racing and gambling.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/indra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indra.jpg" alt="Indra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indra,_Chief_of_the_Gods_LACMA_M.69.13.4_(1_of_5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Worship centred on sacrifice (yajna): the chanting of Rigvedic verses, the singing of Samans and the murmuring of sacrificial formulas (Yajus), with offerings poured into the sacrificial fire, the god Agni, in the belief that whatever was offered there reached the divine. People prayed for rain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sons, long life and heaven. The composers of the hymns, the rishis, were regarded as inspired poets and seers; in post-Vedic times the Veda was understood as śruti, “what is heard”. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> record the liturgy of the 16 or 17 Śrauta priests and the purohitas.</p><p>The main gods were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, Agni and Soma, with gods of social order such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a>–Varuna, Aryaman, Bhaga and Amsa, and nature gods such as Surya (the Sun), Vayu (the wind) and Prithivi (the earth). Goddesses included Ushas (the dawn) and Aditi, and rivers, above all the Saraswati, were also goddesses. The gods were not thought all-powerful: the relationship with them was a transaction, with Agni as messenger between the two. Ethics rested on satya and ṛta, the order that regulates the universe, whose violation brought punishment. The Soma cult and fire worship keep traces of a common Indo-Iranian religion, both preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>.</p><p>In the later period the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a> systematised the religious literature and developed the Śrauta ritual. Its best-known new sacrifice was the Ashvamedha, in which a consecrated horse was set free to roam for a year, followed by warriors; the lands it entered had to pay homage or fight. Vedic people believed in the transmigration of the soul, and by the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a> the peepul tree and the cow were sacred. Later ideas of Indian philosophy such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> and karma trace their roots to the Vedas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rigveda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rigveda.jpg" alt="Rigveda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rigveda_MS2097.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The great achievement of the age was its literature, composed and passed on orally with precision. The four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> are its core, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, the Aranyakas, the older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the oldest Śrautasutras. By their language the texts fall into five layers: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, by far the most archaic and closest to the Indo-Iranian heritage; the mantra texts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaveda">Samaveda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a>; the Samhita prose of the Black Yajurveda; the Brahmana prose, with the oldest Upanishads; and the Sutras, the last stratum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a> before c. 500 BCE. The grammar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Pāṇini</a> marks the final apex of the Sutra texts and the beginning of Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>.</p><p>Music and craft are both recorded. The flute (vana), lute (vina), harp, cymbals and drums were played, using a heptatonic scale. The Rigveda names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-makers, cart-makers, carpenters, metalworkers, tanners, bow-makers, weavers and makers of mats of grass and reed. It also mentions a metal, ayas, used for razors, bangles and axes. Some scholars take ayas to be iron, but philological evidence indicates that in the Rigveda it meant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and bronze, and that iron, “black metal”, is first named in the later Atharvaveda; on this reading the Early Vedic period was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture and the Late Vedic period an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> one. Later texts also mention <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, lead and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><p>Visual art is scarce. Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> has very early images of deities from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, but the following millennium is devoid of such remains. It has been suggested that early Vedic religion, centred on the forces of nature and elaborate sacrifices, did not lend itself to images of gods in human form. Abstract designs on the pottery of the Black and Red Ware (1450–1200 BCE) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware</a> (1200–600 BCE) cultures are among the few examples of artistic expression.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kuru kingdom" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Kuru_Kingdom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roshan Krishna Dhanawade (Srimant ROSHAN), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> describes conflicts between the Aryas and the Dasas and Dasyus, people who did not perform sacrifices or obey the commands of the gods; the words describing their speech and appearance are open to many interpretations. Some modern scholars, such as Asko Parpola, connect the Dasas and Dasyus with the Iranian Dahae and Dahyu and think they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-aryans">Indo-Aryans</a> who arrived before the Vedic Aryans. Wars among the Vedic tribes also appear, above all the Battle of the Ten Kings on the river Parushni (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a>), where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatas">Bharatas</a> under their chief Sudas defeated a confederation of ten tribes. After the war the Bharatas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purus">Purus</a> merged into a new tribe, the Kuru.</p><p>Horses remained a priority for Vedic leaders, a remnant of nomadic life. Because horses needed for cavalry and sacrifice could not be bred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, trade routes were maintained beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. There are references to boats, and Book X of the Rigveda mentions both eastern and western oceans. The Panis of some hymns were merchants; the suggestion that they were Semitic traders rests on slim evidence. Settled farming in the later period brought more trade and more competition for resources, and the Ashvamedha sacrifice put considerable pressure on relations between states.</p><p>The Vedic peoples were not the only Indo-Aryans in northern India. According to Bronkhorst, the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> Plain was dominated by a related but non-Vedic culture, “Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>”, which placed kshatriyas above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and rejected Vedic rituals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a> itself declined after defeat by the non-Vedic Salva tribe. By the 6th century BCE commerce and travel flourished between the new kingdoms, and the Achaemenid invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley under Cyrus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> marked the beginning of outside influence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/upanishads"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upanishads.jpg" alt="Upanishads" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_and_DiscipleI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of the Vedic period was marked by linguistic, cultural and political change. By the 6th century BCE the political units had consolidated into large kingdoms, the Mahajanapadas, true cities were rising, and in the Kosala-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> region the śramaṇa movements, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, challenged the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and their Vedic scriptures and ritual. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Pāṇini</a>’s grammar opened the age of Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. The Mahajanapada period followed, and the culture of Greater Magadha reached its height under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>.</p><p>Vedic ideas lasted far longer. Vedic religion developed into Brahmanical orthodoxy, and around the beginning of the Common Era the Vedic tradition formed one of the main constituents of the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> synthesis”. The old ritual survived in the Śrauta tradition, while Yoga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> acknowledged the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> but read the Vedic pantheon as a unitary view of the universe, with Brahman both immanent and transcendent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the epics, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, were essential to these later developments.</p><p>The hierarchy of social classes that emerged in the period remained influential, and ideas such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> and karma trace their roots to the Vedas. Its chronology is still disputed: the mainstream dating rests on the texts and archaeology, while the Puranic chronology places Vedic events thousands of years earlier, and an indigenous origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-aryans">Indo-Aryans</a>, popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, remains outside the academic mainstream.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">In the Research Series</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State<span>Essay</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Vedic period (c. 1500–500 BCE) is named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, liturgical texts composed and orally transmitted in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> by speakers of an Old Indo-Aryan language. Its society moved from tribal herding in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> to farming and kingdoms such as the Kuru in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> Plain. It produced fire sacrifice, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> hierarchy and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, and its tradition became a main part of later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>.</em></p>

